    stem:'Which of the following is the most common hallucination in schizophrenia?',
    options:['Visual','Tactile','Olfactory','Auditory'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:'**Auditory hallucinations are the commonest hallucinations in schizophrenia, and the only modality the schizophrenia lecture gives a slide of its own.**\n\n`L11,12) Schizophrenia` defines the symptom first: *"Hallucinations are perceptions that occur without an external stimulus. In other words, a person experiences something through their senses even though nothing is actually there. (visual, auditory, olfactory, gustatory, tactile)"*. All five modalities are named on that one line, so the deck does not settle the question by omission — every option here is a real kind of hallucination.\n\nWhat settles it is the slide that follows, headed **"Auditory Hallucinations in Schizophrenia"**, which is the only modality-specific slide in the deck and lists the forms the disorder produces: *"Third-person hallucinations: voices discussing the person"*, *"Running commentary: a voice continuously comments on the person\'s actions"*, *"Command hallucinations: voices instruct the person to do something"* and *"Voices arguing: two or more voices talking about the person."* Every one of the deck\'s five case vignettes turns on voices — the man who says *"The voices told me to do it"*, the man with *"a running commentary on his thoughts"*, the salesman hearing *"derogatory voices from his manager\'s office on the other side of the building."* Not one vignette prints a hallucination in any other modality.\n\n**The ranking itself — that auditory outranks the rest — is not printed in the course material** (the phrase "most common" does not appear in the schizophrenia deck at all); roughly 60–80 % of patients with schizophrenia report auditory hallucinations, *not taken from the course material*.\n\n**The other three modalities, and why each is the wrong pick here:**\n\n| Option | What it is, and why it is not the answer |\n|---|---|\n| **Visual** | A hallucination in sight. `L4,5) Psychiatric symptomatology` prints it under hallucinations with a warning attached — *"Visual …….. Organicity"* — meaning a visual hallucination should push you towards an organic cause (delirium, drug intoxication, withdrawal) rather than towards schizophrenia. It occurs in schizophrenia but is not its commonest form |\n| **Tactile** | Named in the deck\'s modality list, but nowhere developed. Tactile hallucinations — the sensation of insects on or under the skin — are characteristic of stimulant intoxication and alcohol withdrawal rather than of schizophrenia, *not taken from the course material* |\n| **Olfactory** | Also named and not developed. A hallucination of smell classically signals temporal-lobe pathology, and belongs with `L4,5`\'s organicity flags rather than with schizophrenia, *not taken from the course material* |\n| **Auditory** *(the keyed answer)* | The modality the deck singles out with its own slide, its own four-item taxonomy, and every case vignette in the lecture |\n\n**Template pair, not a duplicate:** this question shares three option strings — Visual, Olfactory, Auditory — with `gg-ps-t15` Q5, in a different order and with a different fourth option. The two ask opposite things and the discriminating token is *"most common … in schizophrenia"* here against *"NOT a type"* there: this stem wants the commonest real modality, that one wants the item that is not a modality at all. A shared option menu pairs questions; it never folds them.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
