  {
    id:'npqb-ps-99', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'ps-somato',
    stem:'Hypochondriasis is diagnosed among patients having:',
    options:['Morbid preoccupation and fear of having disease','Morbid delusion of having disease','Morbid occupation of having disease','All of the above'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:'**Hypochondriasis is a morbid *preoccupation with and fear of* having a serious disease — a worry held with partial insight, not a fixed belief held with none.** That distinction is the whole question, and it is what separates option a from option b.\n\n`L3) Somatization` names hypochondriasis as one of the somatoform disorders — its DSM-IV list reads *"Somatization disorder * · Undifferentiated somatoform disorder · Conversion disorder * · Pain disorder · Hypochondriasis * · Body dysmorphic disorder * · Somatoform disorder not otherwise specified (NOS)"*, with the asterisk marking the ones the course examines — and it gives hypochondriasis a slide of its own headed *"(Hypochondriasis)"*. **That slide carries no text in the cached deck**, so the definition itself is *not taken from the course material*: preoccupation with, and fear of, having a serious illness, arising from misinterpretation of ordinary bodily sensations, and persisting despite adequate medical evaluation and reassurance.\n\nWhat the deck does supply is the frame the definition sits in. Somatization is *"a clinical state characterized by symptoms without demonstrable physical abnormality"*, with a *"role of psychological factors in initiating and maintaining symptoms"*, and *"symptoms and health concerns not under the patient\'s conscious control."* That last clause matters twice over: it separates hypochondriasis from factitious illness, which `L3` defines as *"Intentional feigning of physical or psychological symptoms"* where *"Motivation for the behavior is the sick role"*. The deck\'s own opening vignette is a hypochondriacal picture — a woman who *"remains convinced that she has a serious physical illness that remains undiagnosed"* and *"demands further referrals and further investigations."*\n\n**Why each of the other three is wrong:**\n\n- **b. Morbid delusion of having disease.** A delusion is *"False fixed belief, can\'t be reasoned"* (`L4,5) Psychiatric symptomatology`), and it belongs to psychosis. `L4,5` draws the line by insight: *"Intact …. (Anxiety) · Absent .... (Psychosis) · Partial …. (Somatic symptom)."* The hypochondriacal patient has **partial** insight — he can entertain the possibility that he is well, which is why reassurance helps briefly before the fear returns. A patient whose conviction of illness is unshakeable and unreasonable has a somatic delusion, and that is a psychotic disorder, not hypochondriasis.\n- **c. Morbid occupation of having disease.** ⚠️ **Printed exactly as transcribed — the bank prints "occupation", not "preoccupation"**, which option a does print. It is a degraded copy of option a with the prefix dropped, re-read at 400 dpi to be certain. The key A is unaffected: even read charitably, "occupation" is not a psychiatric term and does not state the criterion. Recorded, not corrected.\n- **d. All of the above.** Fails on b alone — a delusion of disease is not hypochondriasis. Aggregate options are only correct when every member is, and this one contains a distractor that inverts the diagnosis.\n\n**The discriminator to carry:** fear and preoccupation with partial insight → hypochondriasis (somatoform); unshakeable conviction with absent insight → somatic delusion (psychotic); deliberate feigning for the sick role → factitious disorder.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'Hypochondriasis is a morbid preoccupation with, and fear of, having a serious disease, held with partial insight and persisting despite medical reassurance — not a delusion of disease, which implies absent insight and belongs to psychosis.',
    source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.82 (answer p.85). DEFECT RECORDED, NOT CORRECTED: option c prints "Morbid occupation of having disease", without the "pre-" that option a carries; re-read at 400 dpi. The key A is unaffected and is transcribed as printed. The cached L3) Somatization slide headed "(Hypochondriasis)" carries no body text, so the definition in the explanation is filled from general medical knowledge and tagged there.'
  },

  {
    id:'npqb-ps-100', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'ps-schizmgmt',
    stem:'The average life expectancy of people with schizophrenia is less than the average life expectancy by:',
    options:['10-15 years','10-25 years','20-30 years','5-10 years'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'**Schizophrenia shortens life by roughly 10 to 25 years — the widest of the four ranges that still begins in the second decade.**\n\n**This figure is not printed anywhere in the cached lectures.** "Life expectancy" appears once in the whole folder, in `L5) Muscle Disease` and about a muscular dystrophy; the schizophrenia deck prints no mortality figure at all. The 10–25 year mortality gap is the World Health Organization\'s figure and is *not taken from the course material*.\n\n**Read the options before you answer — two of them start at 10.** The discriminator is the upper bound, not the lower, which is what makes this a genuine question rather than a recall of one number.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **10-15 years** | Too narrow. It captures the lower half of the published estimates and cuts off the excess mortality seen in chronic, poorly treated and institutionalised patients, *not taken from the course material* |\n| **10-25 years** *(the keyed answer)* | The range as usually quoted — a gap that is wide because the drivers are several and vary between populations |\n| **20-30 years** | Too wide at both ends. A 20-year floor would make even the best outcomes catastrophic, and the deck\'s own figures argue against that: *"1/3 achieve significant recovery, 1/3 partial recovery"* |\n| **5-10 years** | Far too low, and the most dangerous distractor because it sounds moderate. It would put schizophrenia in the range of common chronic physical illness rather than among the most life-shortening psychiatric diagnoses |\n\n**Where the years are lost.** The excess mortality is mostly *physical*, not psychiatric: cardiovascular and metabolic disease, high rates of smoking, obesity and diabetes (some of it antipsychotic-related), and poorer access to and uptake of general medical care; suicide contributes substantially but accounts for a minority of the gap. All of that is *not taken from the course material*.\n\nWhat the decks do supply is the clinical shape of the risk. `L11,12) Schizophrenia` lists the indications for hospitalisation and they read as a catalogue of ways patients die: *"Potential hazard of danger to himself ( suicide & excitement ) or to others ( aggression or homicide)"*, *"Possible deterioration of his health : dehydration ; total refusal of food; no insight & refusal of medications"*, and *"Emergency side effect like : neuroleptic malignant syndrome."* Refusal of food, refusal of medication and absent insight are precisely the mechanisms by which a treatable illness becomes a fatal one, and `L4,5) Psychiatric symptomatology` puts insight at the centre of it — *"Absent .... (Psychosis)"*. `L15) substance related disorder and psychiatry emergency` adds the population figure for the suicide component: *"Suicide accounts for (1 to 2%) of total global mortality (1 in 6000/year)."*\n\n**The point the number is making:** the mortality gap in schizophrenia is largely from ordinary physical disease that is under-detected and under-treated, which is why physical health monitoring is part of psychiatric management and not an afterthought.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'People with schizophrenia die on average 10-25 years earlier than the general population, the gap driven mainly by cardiovascular and metabolic disease, smoking and poor access to general medical care, with suicide contributing a minority share.',
    source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.82 (answer p.85). All four ranges read off 400 dpi crops, not from OCR. No life-expectancy or mortality figure for schizophrenia is printed in the cached lectures; the range and its drivers are filled from general medical knowledge and tagged in the explanation.'
  },

  {
    id:'npqb-ps-101', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'ps-symptom',
    stem:'Illusions are perceptual disturbance that are present when patients:',
    options:['Misinterpret an internal stimulus','Misinterpret without an external stimulus','Misinterpret an external stimulus','Misinterpret without an internal stimulus'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:'**An illusion is the misinterpretation of a stimulus that is really there, outside the patient — so option c is the definition and option b is the definition of a hallucination.**\n\n`L4,5) Psychiatric symptomatology` defines perception as *"Giving a meaning to received stimuli"* and then gives it two abnormalities, separated on the single question of whether a stimulus exists:\n\n| Lecture term | The lecture\'s own words |\n|---|---|\n| **Illusions** | *"False perception for an existing stimulus"* · *"Normal phenomena"* · *"Visual, vivid …….. Organicity"* |\n| **Hallucinations** | *"Non existing stimulus"* · *"Auditory, visual, olfactory, tactile, gustatory"* · *"Auditory (e.g. commenting, commanding, …)"* · *"Visual …….. Organicity"* · *"Normal hallucinations"* |\n\nThe deck\'s fourth matching exercise keys *"False perception for an existing stimulus"* to *h) Illusions*, and `L11,12) Schizophrenia` states the hallucination side independently: *"Hallucinations are perceptions that occur without an external stimulus."* Both decks put the stimulus **outside** the patient. Perception in this scheme is the processing of what comes in; there is no category of "internal stimulus" in it.\n\n**Why each of the other three is wrong:**\n\n- **a. Misinterpret an internal stimulus.** "Internal stimulus" is not a term the course uses for perception. Read generously it would mean a bodily sensation misread as disease, which is the mechanism of hypochondriasis and of somatisation — `L3) Somatization` calls it an *"Amplifying perceptual style"* among its explanations of somatic complaints. That is a disorder of interpretation and belief, not a perceptual disturbance in the sense meant here.\n- **b. Misinterpret without an external stimulus.** This is the hallucination, printed almost word for word: *"Non existing stimulus"*. It is the strongest distractor and the one the question exists to catch, because "without an external stimulus" and "an external stimulus" differ by one word and invert the answer.\n- **d. Misinterpret without an internal stimulus.** Combines both errors — a category the course does not use, negated. Nothing in either deck corresponds to it.\n\n**The lecture\'s two riders are worth keeping.** Illusions are *"Normal phenomena"*: a coat on a chair taken for a person in a dark room is not a symptom, and this is what makes illusions unlike hallucinations, which — the deck\'s *"Normal hallucinations"* aside, meaning hypnagogic and hypnopompic experiences — usually are. And the flag *"Visual, vivid …….. Organicity"* means a vivid visual illusion should send you looking for a medical cause; `L4,5` repeats that logic across its whole organicity block, where *"(non auditory) hallucinations, illusions"* appear together among the complaints that raise suspicion.\n\n**One printed defect, transcribed and not corrected:** the stem reads *"Illusions are perceptual disturbance that are present"* — singular "disturbance" against the plural subject and plural verb. It does not change the meaning or the key.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'An illusion is the misinterpretation of a real, existing external stimulus, while a hallucination is a perception with no external stimulus at all; the lecture also calls illusions normal phenomena and flags vivid visual ones as a pointer to organicity.',
    source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.83 (answer p.85). The stem prints singular "perceptual disturbance" against plural "Illusions ... are"; transcribed as printed, corrected nowhere. Same content as Q96 (npqb-ps-96), which asks it as a vignette rather than a definition, and adjacent to Q102 (npqb-ps-102); flagged as related, not duplicates.'
  },

  {
    id:'npqb-ps-102', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'ps-symptom',
    stem:'Which of the following is an abnormality of perception?',
    options:['Delusion','Hallucination','Insomnia','Perseveration'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:'**`L4,5) Psychiatric symptomatology` gives perception exactly two abnormalities — illusions and hallucinations — and only one of them is on this option list.**\n\nThe deck\'s perception slide reads in full: *"P…Perception: ❖ Giving a meaning to received stimuli ❖Illusions — ✓ False perception for an existing stimulus ✓ Normal phenomena ✓ Visual, vivid …….. Organicity ❖Hallucinations — ✓ Non existing stimulus ✓ Auditory, visual, olfactory, tactile, gustatory ✓ Auditory (e.g. commenting, commanding, …) ✓ Visual …….. Organicity ✓ Normal hallucinations."* Hallucination is there by name. Each of the other three options is filed by the same deck under a different heading, which is what makes this a clean single-answer question.\n\n**Why each of the other three is wrong — and where the lecture puts it instead:**\n\n- **Delusion → thought.** `L4,5` opens the intellectual functions with *"T…Thought: (Type, Content, Form)"* and lists *"Content: delusions, obsessions"*. A delusion is a false belief — *"False fixed belief, can\'t be reasoned, not related to the cultural or religious background of the patient"* — not a false percept. This is the trap option, because delusions and hallucinations occur together constantly in psychosis; the deck still keeps them in different rows.\n- **Insomnia → sleep.** The deck files it under sleep complaints, quantitative decrease: *"Insomnia (early, late)"*, beside *"decrease need for sleep"*, and its first matching exercise distinguishes early from late insomnia by example (*"I sleep on time, but I wake up 3 hours earlier than I used to"* = late insomnia). Nothing perceptual about it.\n- **Preservation → behaviour, and it is a misprint.** ⚠️ **The bank prints "Preservation" where "Perseveration" is plainly meant, and it is transcribed here exactly as printed.** This is the bank\'s own house misspelling — `gg-ps-t15` records five earlier instances, in its Q2b, Q3b, Q5d, Q9b and Q12b — and this is the **sixth**. It is corrected nowhere, because the misprint is itself a real English word and the bank uses it consistently. Read as intended, **perseveration** is *"Repetition of the same response inspite of the change of stimulus"* (`L4,5`\'s third matching exercise), a disorder of behaviour and of thought form; `L4,5` prints *"D.D perseveration ….. Organicity"* immediately after its catatonia list and again lists *"Perseveration"* among the organicity examination signs. Read as printed, "preservation" is not a psychiatric term at all. Either way it is not perceptual, and the key B is unaffected.\n\n**The map to carry**, which answers most of this topic\'s symptomatology questions at once: false belief → thought content · false percept → perception · repetition despite a changed stimulus → behaviour, and a pointer to organicity · disturbed sleep, appetite or libido → the quantitative complaint lists.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'The lecture gives perception two abnormalities only — illusions and hallucinations; delusion belongs to thought content, insomnia to the sleep complaints, and perseveration (printed here as "Preservation") to behaviour, where it also flags organicity.',
    source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.83 (answer p.85). DEFECT RECORDED, NOT CORRECTED: option d prints "Preservation" where "Perseveration" is meant, re-read at 400 dpi. This is the bank\'s house misspelling and its sixth recorded instance; gg-ps-t15.array.js logs five earlier ones in its Q2b, Q3b, Q5d, Q9b and Q12b. The key B is unaffected.'
  },

  {
    id:'npqb-ps-103', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'ps-symptom',
    stem:'A patient, who does not eat because he is convinced that his family is poisoning his food, is said to have:',
    options:['Anorexia','Obsession','Apathy','Delusion'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:'**The behaviour is refusing food; the symptom is the belief that produces it, and a conviction of being poisoned that survives all evidence is a persecutory delusion.**\n\n`L4,5) Psychiatric symptomatology` defines it in its fourth matching exercise — *"False fixed belief, can\'t be reasoned, not related to the cultural or religious background of the patient"* — and files delusions under thought **content**. `L11,12) Schizophrenia` prints the same definition and the matching example: *"Delusions, are fixed false beliefs that a person strongly believes even when there is clear evidence that they are not true"*, with *"Believing that strangers are spying on you without any evidence (persecutory delusion)"* as its first illustration. The deck\'s vignettes are built from exactly this material — a man convinced *"the government is after him, but he cannot explain why"*, another who *"has turned the house upside down as he is convinced that there is a microphone taping his family conversations."* The word *convinced* in the bank\'s stem is doing the same work.\n\n**Why each of the other three is wrong:**\n\n- **Anorexia.** `L4,5` defines it plainly as a quantitative appetite complaint: *"Decrease: Anorexia (loss of appetite)"*, and its first matching exercise keys *"I lost my appetite"* to *d) Anorexia*. This patient has not lost his appetite. He is hungry and refuses to eat for a reason. Anorexia names the wrong level of the problem — it describes what you would see from the end of the bed, not what is wrong with the patient. (Refusal of food is nonetheless dangerous in its own right: `L11,12` lists *"total refusal of food"* among its indications for hospitalisation, beside dehydration and absent insight.)\n- **Obsession.** The strongest distractor, because `L4,5` files obsessions under thought content **alongside** delusions — the same row of the same slide. The split is insight and resistance. `L8) OCD & PTSD` describes obsessions as *"Recurrent, intrusive thoughts, urges, or images"* whose content is *"ego-dystonic"*, with *"attempts to suppress"* them; the obsessional patient knows the thought is his own, finds it senseless and fights it. A delusional patient does none of that — he does not resist the belief, because to him it is simply true. A man with contamination obsessions might also avoid food, but he would tell you his fear is irrational.\n- **Apathy.** An affective symptom. `L4,5` lists *"Blunted affect or apathy"* on the quantitative *decrease* side of mood and affect, beside *"Anhedonia"* and *"Indifference"*, and puts *"Avolition"* among the decreased activity terms. An apathetic patient might not eat because nothing moves him to; this one is moved, and towards a specific fear.\n\n**The reasoning to copy for any vignette of this shape:** name the abnormal *mental phenomenon*, not the behaviour it causes. Not eating is the observation; the fixed unshakeable belief behind it is the sign, and its theme — persecutory here — tells you which delusion it is.\n\nWritten for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'A patient who refuses food because he is convinced his family is poisoning him has a persecutory delusion — a fixed false belief that cannot be reasoned away — and not anorexia (loss of appetite), an obsession (ego-dystonic and resisted) or apathy (loss of feeling and drive).',
    source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.83 (answer p.85)'
  },

  {
    id:'npqb-ps-104', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'ps-schizmgmt',
    stem:'A 35-years old woman has lived in a psychiatric hospital for the past 10 years. She spends most of her day rocking, muttering softly to herself, or looking at her reflections in a small mirror. She needs help with dressing and showering, and she often giggles and laughs for no apparent reason. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?',
    options:['Schizophrenia','Delusional disorder','Bipolar disorder','Schizoaffective disorder','Schizophreniform disorder'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:'The bank prints an explanation box for this question. **Transcribed verbatim as printed:**\n\n> The patient\'s presentation is most consistent with schizophrenia, characterized by: Chronic duration (10 years in this case). Prominent negative symptoms (e.g., social withdrawal, lack of self-care, and flat or inappropriate affect, such as giggling for no reason). Disorganized behavior (e.g., rocking, muttering to herself). Impaired functioning (requiring assistance with daily activities like dressing and showering).\n\n**Note the option count: this question prints FIVE options, a–e.** It and Q98 are the only two in the topic that do; the other twenty-five print four.\n\n**Every element of the vignette is a term from the decks.** The rocking is `L4,5) Psychiatric symptomatology`\'s *"Stereotypy"* — repetitive, non-goal-directed movement — and the mirror-gazing its *"Mannerism"*, both printed in the catatonia list. Muttering softly to herself is what `L11,12) Schizophrenia`\'s fifth vignette records as *"occasionally mutters to himself"* while *"responding to unseen stimuli"*. Giggling for no apparent reason is `L4,5`\'s *"Inappropriate affect (mood incongruity)"*. Needing help with dressing and showering, and ten years in hospital, are the deck\'s decreased-activity terms — *"Avolition"*, *"Social isolation/withdrawal"*, *"Retarded behaviour"*. Taken together the picture is the deck\'s **disorganised** subtype: `L11,12` lists *"Paranoid · Disorganized · Catatonic · Undifferentiated · Residual"*, noting they are *"no longer in DSM-5, but still clinically discussed"*.\n\nIt is also the deck\'s worst-outcome third: *"1/3 achieve significant recovery, 1/3 partial recovery, 1/3 chronic illness with significant disability."* Ten years of institutional care with deficit symptoms is the third group.\n\n**Why each of the other four is wrong. The whole question is a duration-and-symptom-profile discrimination, so take them one at a time:**\n\n| Option | Why it does not fit |\n|---|---|\n| **Delusional disorder** | Requires non-bizarre delusions with *preserved* functioning and without prominent hallucinations, disorganisation or negative symptoms. This woman has no delusion recorded at all, and her functioning has collapsed. *Not taken from the course material* — the term appears nowhere in the cached decks; `L11,12` names only persecutory, grandiose and erotomanic delusions as themes |\n| **Bipolar disorder** | `L9,10) Mood disorders` requires an episode — *"A period of abnormal and persistent elated mood or expansive or irritable mood accompanied by increased goal-directed behavior or energy"* with three or more of *"Inflated self-esteem or grandiosity · Decrease need for sleep · Racing thoughts · Hyper talkativeness · Flight of ideas · Distractibility · Increase in goal-directed behavior"*. None is present, and bipolar disorder runs in episodes with recovery between them, not as ten continuous years of deficit. The giggling is the trap — it looks like elation until you notice it is unprovoked and unaccompanied by any other manic feature, which makes it incongruous affect, not mood |\n| **Schizoaffective disorder** | Needs a full mood episode occurring *concurrently* with the psychosis, plus at least two weeks of delusions or hallucinations in the *absence* of a mood episode. No mood episode is described here at any point. The course names the disorder only as a treatment indication — `L17) Psychopharmacology` lists *"Schizoaffective disorder"* among the indications for antipsychotics and again among those for mood stabilisers — and prints no criteria, so the definition is *not taken from the course material* |\n| **Schizophreniform disorder** | The duration distractor, and the sharpest one. Its symptoms are identical to schizophrenia; it is defined by lasting **1 to 6 months**. Ten years excludes it outright — this is precisely why the box opens with *"Chronic duration (10 years in this case)"*. The term appears nowhere in the cached decks, *not taken from the course material* |\n| **Schizophrenia** *(the keyed answer)* | Chronic course, prominent negative symptoms, disorganised behaviour and impaired functioning — the four the box names |\n\n**The two axes to carry:** *duration* separates schizophreniform (1–6 months) from schizophrenia (6 months and beyond), and *the presence or absence of a mood episode* separates schizophrenia from schizoaffective and bipolar disorder. Delusional disorder is excluded by everything except a delusion.',
    objective:'A chronic ten-year course with prominent negative symptoms, disorganised behaviour and severe functional impairment is schizophrenia; schizophreniform disorder is excluded by duration (1-6 months), schizoaffective and bipolar disorder by the absence of any mood episode, and delusional disorder by the collapse of functioning.',
    source:'neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf p.83 (answer p.85). FIVE options, a-e, counted on the page image; this and Q98 are the only five-option questions in the topic, so the answer index runs against a five-item list. "A 35-years old woman" (sic, for "35-year-old"), "past 10 years" and the Explanation\'s "(10 years in this case)" were each read off 400 dpi crops and are transcribed as printed. The printed Explanation uses a curly apostrophe in "patient\'s"; a straight one is used here.'
  }

